logs/
**/data/

# Vex Business (vex-business/, renamed from CALL) has two real,
# source-tracked directories the generic **/data/ rule above would
# otherwise silently swallow: a Python package literally named `data/`
# (packages/data/ -- Master Directive Section 5's own named layout,
# real ORM models, not runtime state -- caught directly via a real
# empty-wheel build from hatchling's own git-aware packaging, not
# assumed) and `vex-business/data/sample/` (the Master Directive's own
# real seed/demo-data directory -- currently just a real README
# explaining it's honestly empty, not runtime output). Both carved out
# below; Vex Business's own `data/output/` (its real runtime-generated
# directory, see vex-business/.gitignore) stays genuinely gitignored.
#
# **These paths said `call/` for two weeks after the rename, and that
# is how six real source files sat untracked.** The carve-outs were
# correct when written; `call/` became `vex-business/`, the negations
# did not follow, and `**/data/` quietly reclaimed
# `packages/data/data/orm.py`, `database.py`, `__init__.py`,
# `py.typed`, `pyproject.toml` and `data/sample/README.md`. Nothing
# failed. `git status` was clean, because ignored files are not
# untracked files -- they are invisible. They were absent from every
# release archive and would have been lost with the container.
#
# A negation pattern that names a path is a claim that the path exists.
# `scripts/test/gitignore-carveouts.test.mjs` now checks that claim on
# every run, so the next rename fails a test instead of a restore.
!vex-business/packages/data/
!vex-business/packages/data/data/
!vex-business/data/
!vex-business/data/sample/

# Real secrets, and a real near-miss rather than a precaution.
#
# `deploy/README.md` and `dev-docs/DEPLOYMENT_INVENTORY.md` both tell a
# deployer to run `cp .env.example .env` and fill in
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. Nothing here ignored the result, so the very next
# `git add -A` would have committed a live key -- and this repo has 17
# `.env.example` templates, so that path exists in seventeen places,
# not one. Caught while booting v4-proxy, which is the one service that
# hard-fails without the key.
#
# `.env.example` files stay tracked on purpose: they are the templates,
# and they carry no values.
.env
**/.env
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Store snapshots from scripts/backup-stores.mjs, if the destination
# is ever pointed inside the repo. The default is ../vaco-backups,
# deliberately outside it -- a backup committed alongside the thing it
# backs up protects against nothing.
vaco-backups/

# Release archives built by scripts/package-release.mjs.
#
# Ignored rather than tracked for two reasons. A 3.8MB binary that
# changes wholesale every commit is the wrong thing to keep in git
# history; and the script refuses to package a dirty tree, so an
# untracked archive sitting in the root would make the *next* run
# refuse itself. It is a build output -- rebuild it, do not store it.
vaco-*.tar.gz

# Git bundles (`git bundle create <f> --all`). Same argument, and one
# more: a bundle of the repository committed *into* the repository is
# a snapshot of everything up to the moment before it existed, which
# grows without bound and protects against nothing. Its whole value is
# being carried off this machine.
vaco-*.bundle
